WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.000
On a few nights each year, many of

00:00:02.040 --> 00:00:04.240
the corals on the Great Barrier Reef spawn

00:00:04.320 --> 00:00:05.040
together.

00:00:05.360 --> 00:00:07.760
They release bundles that float up and break

00:00:07.840 --> 00:00:11.360
apart, setting free millions of tiny eggs and

00:00:11.440 --> 00:00:13.880
clouds of sperm in the open sea.

00:00:14.320 --> 00:00:16.160
Now think about a harbour seal on a

00:00:16.240 --> 00:00:17.120
rocky shore.

00:00:17.600 --> 00:00:20.080
In one year she makes a single egg,

00:00:20.160 --> 00:00:22.400
and months later, a single pup.

00:00:22.720 --> 00:00:25.200
So why would one animal release millions of

00:00:25.280 --> 00:00:27.440
eggs, while the other gets by with one?

00:00:28.160 --> 00:00:30.160
By the end, you'll be able to explain

00:00:30.240 --> 00:00:31.680
it in an exam answer.

00:00:32.900 --> 00:00:35.460
Start with the job both animals are doing:

00:00:35.540 --> 00:00:37.540
getting a sperm to meet an egg.

00:00:37.780 --> 00:00:39.940
A sperm cell and an egg cell are

00:00:40.060 --> 00:00:40.820
gametes.

00:00:41.060 --> 00:00:42.980
Last time you saw that a gamete carries

00:00:43.140 --> 00:00:46.260
one set of chromosomes, so one set plus

00:00:46.340 --> 00:00:48.820
one set makes a full two sets.

00:00:49.300 --> 00:00:51.900
Put simply, each gamete carries half of the

00:00:51.940 --> 00:00:53.940
instructions for building a new animal.

00:00:54.420 --> 00:00:56.740
When a sperm joins an egg, that's called

00:00:56.900 --> 00:00:58.060
fertilisation.

00:00:58.260 --> 00:01:00.980
It makes one new cell, the zygote, with

00:01:01.020 --> 00:01:03.140
the full set of instructions, and it will

00:01:03.300 --> 00:01:04.900
grow into the new animal.

00:01:05.140 --> 00:01:06.900
The hard part is getting the two to

00:01:07.060 --> 00:01:07.620
meet.

00:01:07.860 --> 00:01:09.380
An egg can't move.

00:01:09.700 --> 00:01:11.780
A sperm can only swim a short way.

00:01:12.100 --> 00:01:13.940
And both dry out quickly.

00:01:14.180 --> 00:01:17.140
Animals solve this in two main ways, depending

00:01:17.220 --> 00:01:18.660
on where the gametes meet.

00:01:19.867 --> 00:01:22.746
The first way is external fertilisation.

00:01:23.227 --> 00:01:25.386
The sperm and egg meet outside the bodies

00:01:25.467 --> 00:01:27.786
of both parents, and it usually happens in

00:01:27.867 --> 00:01:28.626
water.

00:01:28.987 --> 00:01:30.746
Water matters for two reasons.

00:01:31.147 --> 00:01:34.106
It stops the gametes drying out, and sperm

00:01:34.187 --> 00:01:35.066
need it to swim.

00:01:35.707 --> 00:01:38.026
Corals release their gametes into the sea.

00:01:38.427 --> 00:01:40.506
Frogs do it in a pond, and salmon

00:01:40.587 --> 00:01:42.266
do it in a river, where the female

00:01:42.347 --> 00:01:44.026
lays her eggs in the gravel and the

00:01:44.107 --> 00:01:45.946
male releases sperm over them.

00:01:47.167 --> 00:01:49.406
But water alone isn't enough.

00:01:49.647 --> 00:01:51.166
The gametes have to be in the same

00:01:51.247 --> 00:01:53.486
place at the same time, or they never

00:01:53.567 --> 00:01:54.246
meet.

00:01:54.607 --> 00:01:57.086
Many corals solve this by spawning together on

00:01:57.127 --> 00:01:59.486
the same few nights, so the gametes are

00:01:59.647 --> 00:02:01.406
crowded into one patch of sea.

00:02:02.287 --> 00:02:04.606
Salmon follow the scent of their home river,

00:02:04.687 --> 00:02:06.446
so they gather in the same place, and

00:02:06.487 --> 00:02:08.526
they return at the same time of year.

00:02:09.327 --> 00:02:11.566
Frogs gather in the same pond, and the

00:02:11.647 --> 00:02:14.126
male clasps the female, so the sperm are

00:02:14.367 --> 00:02:15.966
released right over the eggs.

00:02:17.167 --> 00:02:19.806
Even so, the odds are poor, and there

00:02:19.887 --> 00:02:21.726
are two kinds of loss.

00:02:21.967 --> 00:02:24.526
First, many eggs and sperm are lost before

00:02:24.607 --> 00:02:25.646
they meet.

00:02:25.847 --> 00:02:29.086
Currents sweep gametes away, hungry animals eat them,

00:02:29.127 --> 00:02:30.926
and the sperm are spread so thin in

00:02:31.007 --> 00:02:33.326
all that water that many never find an

00:02:33.407 --> 00:02:34.366
egg.

00:02:34.607 --> 00:02:37.486
Second, most of the eggs that are fertilised,

00:02:37.567 --> 00:02:39.246
and the young that grow from them, are

00:02:39.327 --> 00:02:42.206
eaten or die before they can breed.

00:02:42.287 --> 00:02:45.086
A female Chinook salmon lays around four thousand

00:02:45.207 --> 00:02:47.326
to fourteen thousand eggs.

00:02:47.407 --> 00:02:49.966
With that many, a few still make it.

00:02:50.007 --> 00:02:53.406
So the rule is: water, timing and huge

00:02:53.487 --> 00:02:54.206
numbers.

00:02:55.434 --> 00:02:58.713
The second way is internal fertilisation.

00:02:58.754 --> 00:03:01.753
The male places sperm inside the female's body,

00:03:01.794 --> 00:03:03.993
and the egg is fertilised there.

00:03:04.074 --> 00:03:06.473
The gametes now meet in a moist, protected

00:03:06.554 --> 00:03:08.713
space, so they can't dry out and they

00:03:08.794 --> 00:03:10.153
can't be eaten.

00:03:10.194 --> 00:03:12.073
The sperm only has to swim a short

00:03:12.154 --> 00:03:13.273
distance.

00:03:13.434 --> 00:03:15.913
Almost every egg is fertilised, so she needs

00:03:16.034 --> 00:03:17.673
far fewer eggs.

00:03:17.754 --> 00:03:19.993
Males still make plenty of sperm.

00:03:20.034 --> 00:03:22.713
The number that matters is eggs.

00:03:22.794 --> 00:03:25.193
This is the way mammals, birds and reptiles

00:03:25.274 --> 00:03:27.033
do it, and it lets them breed on

00:03:27.154 --> 00:03:29.993
dry land, far from standing water.

00:03:30.074 --> 00:03:31.833
Some animals that live in the sea, like

00:03:31.914 --> 00:03:33.713
sharks, use it too.

00:03:33.754 --> 00:03:35.753
The cost is that the parents usually have

00:03:35.834 --> 00:03:37.993
to find each other and mate, and that

00:03:38.074 --> 00:03:39.833
takes time and energy.

00:03:41.034 --> 00:03:41.913
Here's a trap.

00:03:42.394 --> 00:03:44.153
A hen lays an egg with a hard

00:03:44.234 --> 00:03:45.993
shell, outside her body.

00:03:46.474 --> 00:03:48.713
So is that external fertilisation?

00:03:49.274 --> 00:03:49.673
No.

00:03:50.154 --> 00:03:52.393
The sperm has to reach the egg inside

00:03:52.474 --> 00:03:54.873
the hen, before the shell forms around it.

00:03:55.514 --> 00:03:57.993
Fertilisation is about where the sperm and egg

00:03:58.154 --> 00:04:00.473
meet, not where the young one grows.

00:04:01.114 --> 00:04:04.233
So a hen fertilises internally, then lays the

00:04:04.314 --> 00:04:06.793
egg, and the chick develops outside her.

00:04:07.274 --> 00:04:09.673
A seal does both inside her, and a

00:04:09.754 --> 00:04:11.673
frog does both outside the body.

00:04:12.154 --> 00:04:14.713
Two different questions, two different answers.

00:04:15.934 --> 00:04:18.573
Every parent has a limited supply of energy

00:04:18.654 --> 00:04:19.373
and time.

00:04:19.934 --> 00:04:22.253
In mammals and birds, the young begin their

00:04:22.334 --> 00:04:25.213
life inside or beside the mother, so she

00:04:25.294 --> 00:04:26.653
can protect and feed them.

00:04:27.294 --> 00:04:30.493
Protected young survive more often, so each one

00:04:30.574 --> 00:04:32.533
is worth more of her energy.

00:04:32.974 --> 00:04:35.613
A salmon spends hers on thousands of eggs

00:04:35.934 --> 00:04:37.533
and then has nothing left to give her

00:04:37.614 --> 00:04:38.653
young.

00:04:38.734 --> 00:04:41.293
A seal spends hers on a single pup,

00:04:41.374 --> 00:04:43.933
which she carries through months of pregnancy, then

00:04:44.014 --> 00:04:46.013
feeds with milk and guards for a few

00:04:46.214 --> 00:04:47.293
weeks.

00:04:47.414 --> 00:04:50.253
That time, energy and protection a parent gives

00:04:50.334 --> 00:04:53.133
to help its young survive is called parental

00:04:53.214 --> 00:04:54.733
investment.

00:04:54.774 --> 00:04:57.453
So the usual pattern is: many eggs and

00:04:57.534 --> 00:05:00.733
little care, or few young and a lot

00:05:00.774 --> 00:05:01.373
of care.

00:05:02.254 --> 00:05:04.813
Some reptiles bury their eggs and leave, so

00:05:04.894 --> 00:05:06.973
this is a trend, not a rule.

00:05:07.454 --> 00:05:10.653
External fertilisation suits animals that live and breed

00:05:10.814 --> 00:05:11.613
in water.

00:05:12.834 --> 00:05:14.353
Let's build a model answer.

00:05:14.834 --> 00:05:18.753
The question: explain why fish that fertilise externally

00:05:18.994 --> 00:05:21.713
release far more eggs than mammals that fertilise

00:05:21.794 --> 00:05:22.833
internally.

00:05:23.154 --> 00:05:26.993
The command word explain means give reasons, joined

00:05:27.074 --> 00:05:28.113
by because.

00:05:28.754 --> 00:05:30.033
First, the cause.

00:05:30.674 --> 00:05:33.233
Fish release far more eggs because many eggs

00:05:33.274 --> 00:05:35.633
and sperm in water are swept away or

00:05:35.674 --> 00:05:38.033
eaten, and most of the young that form

00:05:38.194 --> 00:05:40.593
are eaten or die before they can breed.

00:05:41.234 --> 00:05:42.513
Next, the effect.

00:05:43.074 --> 00:05:45.633
Fish cope because huge numbers of eggs make

00:05:45.714 --> 00:05:48.113
up for these losses, so some young still

00:05:48.194 --> 00:05:48.833
survive.

00:05:49.394 --> 00:05:50.513
Then the contrast.

00:05:51.074 --> 00:05:53.873
Mammals need far fewer eggs because the sperm

00:05:53.954 --> 00:05:56.193
is placed inside the female, where it is

00:05:56.474 --> 00:05:59.393
protected, so almost every egg is fertilised.

00:05:59.874 --> 00:06:01.953
And they have only a few young because

00:06:02.034 --> 00:06:04.753
each one gets a lot of parental investment.

00:06:05.074 --> 00:06:07.793
Finish with a link: a parent has limited

00:06:07.874 --> 00:06:10.353
energy, so it makes many eggs with little

00:06:10.434 --> 00:06:12.673
care, or few eggs with a lot of

00:06:12.754 --> 00:06:13.233
care.

00:06:14.434 --> 00:06:16.473
That pattern has a consequence.

00:06:16.754 --> 00:06:19.073
Imagine a storm year that kills nine in

00:06:19.154 --> 00:06:21.393
every ten of the young alive, for a

00:06:21.474 --> 00:06:24.113
seal colony and for a cod population.

00:06:24.434 --> 00:06:28.033
Cod fertilise externally and breed year after year,

00:06:28.114 --> 00:06:30.353
and a large female cod can lay millions

00:06:30.434 --> 00:06:31.473
of eggs.

00:06:31.554 --> 00:06:33.873
In both groups the adults survive and breed

00:06:34.034 --> 00:06:36.433
again next year, so the question is how

00:06:36.514 --> 00:06:38.753
many young each adult adds.

00:06:38.954 --> 00:06:41.473
Before the storm, ten seal mothers have ten

00:06:41.554 --> 00:06:44.353
living pups, and each large female cod's eggs

00:06:44.474 --> 00:06:47.713
have already become thousands of living young fish.

00:06:47.874 --> 00:06:50.193
After the storm, the seals have one pup

00:06:50.274 --> 00:06:52.593
left for the ten mothers, and each cod

00:06:52.674 --> 00:06:54.833
has hundreds of young fish left.

00:06:54.954 --> 00:06:57.633
Next breeding season, each seal mother adds only

00:06:57.714 --> 00:07:01.153
about one pup, so the colony rebuilds slowly.

00:07:01.394 --> 00:07:04.113
Each cod adds millions of eggs again, so

00:07:04.234 --> 00:07:06.193
many more young fish can follow, and the

00:07:06.474 --> 00:07:09.313
population can rebuild faster, as long as enough

00:07:09.394 --> 00:07:10.513
adults survive.

00:07:11.734 --> 00:07:12.613
Your turn.

00:07:12.854 --> 00:07:14.253
Three situations.

00:07:14.534 --> 00:07:17.093
One: a lizard lives in a hot, dry

00:07:17.174 --> 00:07:19.013
desert with no standing water.

00:07:19.494 --> 00:07:23.173
Would you expect external or internal fertilisation, and

00:07:23.254 --> 00:07:23.653
why?

00:07:24.374 --> 00:07:26.933
Two: a sea turtle lays her eggs in

00:07:27.014 --> 00:07:28.733
a sand nest on a beach.

00:07:29.014 --> 00:07:31.013
Where did the sperm and egg meet?

00:07:31.334 --> 00:07:34.373
Three: oysters release millions of eggs into the

00:07:34.454 --> 00:07:36.853
sea, while a dugong has a single calf

00:07:36.934 --> 00:07:39.333
and looks after it for a long time.

00:07:39.374 --> 00:07:41.573
A storm kills nine in every ten of

00:07:41.627 --> 00:07:44.133
the young alive in both populations, and the

00:07:44.214 --> 00:07:46.133
adults survive in both.

00:07:46.214 --> 00:07:49.173
Which would recover more slowly, and why?

00:07:49.294 --> 00:07:51.093
Pause the video if you'd like more time.

00:08:26.500 --> 00:08:28.020
One, internal.

00:08:28.300 --> 00:08:31.620
With no standing water, gametes would dry out,

00:08:31.700 --> 00:08:33.780
so the sperm must be placed inside the

00:08:33.860 --> 00:08:35.660
female, where it's moist.

00:08:35.940 --> 00:08:39.060
If you said external because lizards lay eggs,

00:08:39.140 --> 00:08:41.300
hold on to that trap: egg laying is

00:08:41.380 --> 00:08:44.100
about where the young grow, not where fertilisation

00:08:44.180 --> 00:08:44.900
happened.

00:08:45.220 --> 00:08:48.260
Two, inside the female, before the eggs were

00:08:48.300 --> 00:08:48.660
laid.

00:08:49.060 --> 00:08:51.060
If you said outside because the eggs are

00:08:51.140 --> 00:08:53.540
on a beach, it's the same slip.

00:08:53.740 --> 00:08:55.140
Three, the dugong.

00:08:55.540 --> 00:08:58.180
After the storm, each female adds about one

00:08:58.260 --> 00:09:00.180
calf at a time, so even though the

00:09:00.260 --> 00:09:03.460
adults survive, the population climbs back slowly.

00:09:03.940 --> 00:09:06.420
Each oyster adds millions of eggs again next

00:09:06.620 --> 00:09:09.140
season, so many more young can follow, even

00:09:09.220 --> 00:09:10.660
after heavy losses of young.

00:09:11.140 --> 00:09:13.540
If you said oysters recover slower because they

00:09:13.620 --> 00:09:16.980
lose so many eggs, remember: losing many eggs

00:09:17.060 --> 00:09:19.060
is not what slows recovery.

00:09:19.220 --> 00:09:21.540
Having few young per parent is.

00:09:22.767 --> 00:09:25.086
So, two ways to bring sperm and egg

00:09:25.167 --> 00:09:25.806
together.

00:09:26.047 --> 00:09:29.886
External: water and numbers, meaning lots of eggs,

00:09:29.967 --> 00:09:30.846
and good timing.

00:09:31.327 --> 00:09:33.886
Internal: a mate and protection.

00:09:34.367 --> 00:09:37.886
And remember, fertilisation is where they meet, not

00:09:38.047 --> 00:09:39.006
where the young grow.

00:09:39.967 --> 00:09:42.446
Next, we'll look at how plants, fungi and

00:09:42.527 --> 00:09:44.046
microbes reproduce.

00:09:45.267 --> 00:09:46.706
Here are the key notes.

00:09:46.947 --> 00:09:48.866
Pause the video if you need more time

00:09:48.947 --> 00:09:49.746
to copy them.
